        "slug": "webhook-delays",
        "components": [
          "webhooks"
        ],
        "incident": {
          "title": "Delayed webhook delivery",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        "daysAgo": 3,
        "resolvedAfterHours": 1.75,
        "updates": [
          {
            "hoursAfter": 0,
            "body": "Some webhook events are reaching endpoints up to ten minutes late. Payments themselves are not affected, and no event will be lost.",
            "state": "investigating"
          },
          {
            "hoursAfter": 0.6,
            "body": "A delivery queue in one region was under-provisioned after a traffic spike. We have added workers.",
            "state": "identified"
          },
          {
            "hoursAfter": 1.2,
            "body": "The backlog has cleared and new events are delivered within seconds.",
            "state": "monitoring"
          },
          {
            "hoursAfter": 1.75,
            "body": "Resolved. Every delayed event has been delivered.",
            "state": "resolved"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "slug": "card-auth-errors",
        "components": [
          {
            "key": "card-auth",
            "impact": "partial_outage"
          },
          {
            "key": "api",
            "impact": "degraded"
          }
        ],
        "incident": {
          "title": "Elevated card authorisation errors for one network",
          "severity": "major",
          "markdown": "## Impact\n\nFor 38 minutes, about 4% of card authorisations on one card network failed with a processing error. Customers could retry, and most retries succeeded. Other networks, bank debits and payouts were unaffected.\n\n## Timeline\n\n1. 09:14 UTC — authorisation errors rise on one network\n2. 09:19 UTC — our error-rate alert fires\n3. 09:31 UTC — traffic moves to a second connection to the network\n4. 09:52 UTC — the error rate is back to normal\n\n## What caused it\n\nA certificate on one of our connections to the network was rotated on their side before ours, so a share of requests on that connection were refused.\n\n## What we changed\n\nWe now monitor every network connection’s certificate dates, and fail over to a second connection automatically when one starts refusing requests.\n"
        },
        "daysAgo": 11,
        "resolvedAfterHours": 0.65,
        "updates": [
          {
            "hoursAfter": 0,
            "body": "We are seeing elevated authorisation errors for one card network. We are investigating.",
            "state": "investigating"
          },
          {
            "hoursAfter": 0.3,
            "body": "The errors come from one of our connections to the network. We are moving traffic to the other.",
            "state": "identified"
          },
          {
            "hoursAfter": 0.65,
            "body": "Authorisation rates are back to normal on every network. Resolved.",
            "state": "resolved"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "slug": "payout-reports",
        "components": [
          "dashboard"
        ],
        "incident": {
          "title": "Payout reports loading slowly in the dashboard",
          "severity": "minor"
        },
        "daysAgo": 27,
        "resolvedAfterHours": 4,
        "updates": [
          {
            "hoursAfter": 0,
            "body": "Payout reports are taking a long time to load, or not loading. Payouts themselves are on schedule.",
            "state": "investigating"
          },
          {
            "hoursAfter": 2,
            "body": "A reporting database is rebuilding an index. Reports will speed up as it finishes.",
            "state": "identified"
          },
          {
            "hoursAfter": 4,
            "body": "Reports load normally again. Resolved.",
            "state": "resolved"
          }
        ]
      }
